 > [!NOTE]
 > The `secure: true` option tells the browser to only store and send the cookie over HTTPS. When developing locally over plain HTTP, compliant browsers (notably Safari and iOS, and Chrome on some local domains) silently drop the cookie, so the session will not persist. Set `cookie: { secure: false }` during local development to work around this.
 
+## Rotating the password
+
+Changing `password` invalidates every session sealed with the old one. To rotate without signing users out, keep the previous passwords in `extraUnsealPasswords`:
+
+```js
+const session = await useSession(event, {
+  password: process.env.SESSION_PASSWORD_NEW,
+  extraUnsealPasswords: [process.env.SESSION_PASSWORD_OLD],
+});
+```
+
+- `password` is the only one used to seal. The extra passwords only open sessions.
+- Extra passwords are tried in order after `password` fails, so list the most recent first.
+- A session opened with an extra password is resealed with `password` in the same request, so once the old sessions have migrated you can remove the extra password.
+- Keep the list short: each attempt on an invalid cookie costs a PBKDF2 derivation. Extra passwords need at least 32 characters like `password`; a shorter one that is reached makes the session be discarded.
+- Only cookie-based sessions are resealed. A session sent through the session header is read but not migrated, so keep the old password while such clients exist.
+
 ## Expiration
 
 Sessions have two independent expiration controls, and you can use either or both:
